On 22 June 1974, [[East Germany national football team|East Germany]] played [[West Germany national football team|West Germany]] in [[Hamburg]] as part of the [[1974 FIFA World Cup#Group stage|first group stage]] of the [[1974 FIFA World Cup]]. East Germany won the match 1–0, in what is regarded as one of the greatest [[upset (sport)|upsets]] in [[FIFA World Cup]] history.<ref name="y488">{{cite web | title=When East Germany met West and caused one of the greatest World Cup shocks | website=The Independent | first=Adam | last=Bushby | date=2022-11-23 | url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/east-germany-west-germany-1974-world-cup-b2225390.html | access-date=2024-08-18}}</ref> The match is also the only time East and West Germany ever played each other in top-level senior international competition.

==Background==
Going into the match, both East and West Germany were already assured of progressing to the [[1974 FIFA World Cup#Second round|second round]]. East Germany had defeated [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]] 2–0 and drawn with [[Chile national football team|Chile]] 1–1. Host nation West Germany had beaten Chile 1–0 and Australia 3–0, which some fans had seen as unconvincing.<ref name="y488"/> West Germany were considered by pundits to be clear favorites.<ref name="r583">{{cite web | last=Thacker | first=Gary | title=The most politically-charged match in history: when East Germany met West in 1974 | website=These Football Times | date=2018-09-20 | url=https://thesefootballtimes.co/2018/09/21/the-most-politically-charged-match-in-history-when-east-germany-met-west-in-1974/ | access-date=2024-08-18}}</ref>

According to journalist [[Ulrich Hesse]], West German players knew that their manager [[Helmut Schön]], who had been born in [[Dresden]] in East Germany, "wanted – no, needed – to win this match at all costs".<ref name="y488"/> The East German government viewed the match as a [[class struggle]] and politically significant, according to author Thomas Blees.<ref name="y975">{{cite web | title=Als die DDR gegen die BRD bei der Fußball-WM 1974 siegt | website=Deutschlandfunk Kultur | date=2024-06-02 | url=https://www.deutschlandfunkkultur.de/ddr-brd-fussballspiel-als-klassenkampf-100.html | language=de | access-date=2024-08-18}}</ref>

The senior national teams of East and West Germany had never met before, though the countries did play each other at the [[Football at the 1972 Summer Olympics|1972 Summer Olympics]] in [[Munich]], where West Germany was represented by an [[amateur]] side. East Germany won this game 3–2.<ref name="y975"/>

==Match==

===Summary===
West Germany dominated the early chances, though East Germany also had scoring opportunities, including by [[Hans-Jürgen Kreische]] who missed an open goal at close range. West German [[Gerd Müller]] subsequently hit the post. In the 77th minute, East German [[Jürgen Sparwasser]] received a long diagonal pass and ran toward the West German goal, shooting the ball over goalkeeper [[Sepp Maier]] into the roof of the net. Sparwasser did a [[somersault]] in celebration, and later said it was the first time he had ever done one.<ref name="y488"/>

Around 1,500 East German citizens attended the match, including hundreds of [[Stasi]] members, who were instructed to cheer on East Germany with the chant "7, 8, 9, 10, Klasse!" ("7, 8, 9, 10, Great!").<ref name="Hawley 2006">{{cite web | last=Hawley | first=Charles | title=Germany's Other National Team: 1974 East Germans Take to the Field | website=DER SPIEGEL | date=2006-06-23 | url=https://www.spiegel.de/international/germany-s-other-national-team-1974-east-germans-take-to-the-field-a-423236.html | access-date=2024-01-18}}</ref><ref name="Deutschlandfunk Kultur 2014">{{cite web | first=Frank | last=Ulbricht | title="…7, 8, 9, 10, Klasse!" | trans-title=…7, 8, 9, 10, Great! | website=Deutschlandfunk Kultur | date=2014-06-22 | url=https://www.deutschlandfunkkultur.de/fussball-7-8-9-10-klasse-100.html | language=de | access-date=2024-01-18}}</ref>

