==Aftermath==
[[File:Wimpel WM 1974 DDR.jpg|thumb|upright|Pennant commemorating the East German 1974 World Cup team in DDR musuem.]]
The players of both teams [[Shirt swapping|swapped shirts]] after the match, though not on the pitch as this was officially forbidden.<ref name="y488"/>

According to West German striker [[Gerd Müller]], "all hell broke loose" in the West German training camp after the loss.<ref name="a925"/> East Germany's win gave them the top spot in their group, but paradoxically resulted in them getting a more difficult group in the second round. East Germany was eliminated in the second round, while West Germany went on to win the entire tournament.<ref name="BBC"/><ref name="i294">{{cite web | title=WM 1974 in Hamburg: Die DDR schlägt den "Klassenfeind" | website=NDR.de | date=2024-06-24 | url=https://www.ndr.de/geschichte/chronologie/WM-1974-in-Hamburg-Die-DDR-schlaegt-Klassenfeind,fussballwm112.html | language=de | access-date=2024-08-18}}</ref>

Sparwasser became a national hero in East Germany, with his goal simply being known as "The Sparwasser Goal".<ref name="Hawley 2006"/> In 1988, Sparwasser defected to West Germany.<ref name="i294"/><ref name="h026">{{cite web | title=Die Stasi und der Fußball | website=Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung | date=2006-05-21 | url=https://www.faz.net/aktuell/sport/fussball-wm-2006/deutschland-und-die-wm/leben-in-der-unfreiheit-1332249.html | language=de | trans-title=The Stasi and Football | access-date=2025-07-03 | archive-url=https://archive.today/20241006022924/https://www.faz.net/aktuell/sport/fussball-wm-2006/deutschland-und-die-wm/leben-in-der-unfreiheit-1332249.html | archive-date=2024-10-06}}</ref> Sparwasser later said that "If one day my gravestone simply says 'Hamburg 74', everybody will still know who is lying below".<ref name="a925">{{cite web | title= The capstone for Croy and the East | website=FIFA | url=https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/articles/jurgen-croy-east-germany | date=2024-06-22 | access-date=2024-08-18}}</ref> East Germans used to commonly ask each other "Where were you when Sparwasser scored?".<ref name="n471">{{cite web | title=World Cup'74: W. Germany 0-1 E. Germany—Where Were You When Sparwasser Scored? | website=Bleacher Report | date=2008-10-27 | url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/73829-world-cup74-w-germany-0-1-e-germany-where-were-you-when-sparwasser-scored | access-date=2024-08-18}}</ref>

East German striker [[Hans-Jürgen Kreische]] met West German politician [[Hans Apel]] on a flight immediately after the game, and the two men made an informal bet  – Kreische correctly predicted the West would win the trophy and Apel sent him some [[whisky]], unaware of the difficulties this would cause. Kreische later found he had been excluded from the subsequent national squad for the [[1976 Summer Olympics|1976 Olympic Games]] due to the Stasi being aware of his correspondence with Apel, which was forbidden by the East German authorities.<ref name="BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47456049 |title=World Cup whisky and the Cold War: When East & West Germany met |first=Mani |last=Djazmi |publisher=BBC Sport |date=7 March 2019 |access-date=8 March 2019}}</ref>

The match is considered to be one of the most politically charged to ever take place in the World Cup.<ref name="k285">{{cite web | last=Smith | first=Rob | title=Here are 4 of the most politically charged World Cup games ever played | website=World Economic Forum | date=2018-06-13 | url=https://www.weforum.org/agenda/2018/06/here-are-4-of-the-most-politically-charged-world-cup-games-ever-played-russia2018/ | access-date=2024-09-15}}</ref> East and West Germany would never play each other again.<ref name="p466">{{cite web | last=Mansel | first=Tim | title=The East German team that refused to die | website=BBC News | date=2015-12-28 | url=https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-35169108 | access-date=2024-09-15}}</ref>

